<div class="task-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">Make the work usable</span><p>Accessible work is easier for everyone to read. Build it in as you go, then check the file you actually share.</p></div></div><ol class="guide-steps"><li><h3>Use real headings and clear links</h3><p>Use the template’s heading styles rather than bold text, and write links that say where they go. Screen readers rely on both to find their way around.</p></li><li><h3>Describe images that carry meaning</h3><p>Give informative images alternative text that explains what they show and why they’re there. Purely decorative images should be marked as decorative so they’re skipped.</p></li><li><h3>Caption video and transcribe audio</h3><p>Add captions to video and offer a text version of any audio, so the content works with the sound off.</p></li><li><h3>Make text easy to read</h3><p>Check contrast: WCAG’s minimum is generally 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text. Don’t rely on colour alone; add labels, symbols or patterns too.</p></li><li><h3>Check the file you share</h3><p>Accessibility can be lost on export, so check the PDF or published version too: reading order, alternative text, and keyboard access and visible focus for anything interactive.</p></li></ol><section class="reading-section"><h2>The full standard</h2><p>These checks are a starting point, not a claim that the work passes a full accessibility audit. The standard itself has every requirement.</p><ul class="link-cards"><li class="link-card"><span class="link-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 5.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 3H20v15H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 0 4 20.5z"/><path d="M4 20.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 0 6.5 23H20v-5"/></svg></span><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/" class="link-title">WCAG 2.2 quick reference <span class="arr arr-out" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><span class="link-desc">Every requirement in the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines, with techniques for meeting each one.</span><span class="link-where">W3C · w3.org</span></li></ul></section><nav class="page-pager" aria-label="Previous and next in How-to guides"><a class="pager-prev" href="$link.page('CRH', 'Print Supplier Contacts')" rel="prev"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span>Work with print suppliers</a><a class="pager-next" href="$link.page('CRH', 'Adobe Firefly')" rel="next">Using AI image tools<span class="arr" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></nav><nav class="section-next" aria-label="More in How-to guides"><div class="section-next-head"><h2>More in How-to guides</h2><a class="related-all" href="$link.page('CRH', 'Self-Service Guides')">Self-service guides <span class="arr" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="$link.page('CRH', 'Adobe Firefly')"><strong>Using AI image tools</strong><span>Use the current SKAO AI policy and approved tools before starting a task.</span></a><a class="related-card" href="$link.page('CRH', 'Making Your Own Poster')"><strong>Prepare a poster</strong><span>Most of the work on a poster happens before you open a template: knowing the size you need and the one thing you want people to remember.</span></a><a class="related-card" href="$link.page('CRH', 'Preparing Files for Print')"><strong>Prepare files for print</strong><span>Every supplier and product is a little different, so set up and export your file to the supplier’s specification rather than a one-size-fits-all setting.</span></a></div></nav>
